{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792316014,"nonce":"wWXtcyv55PZKyXQTuE6uMnqk+6E=","title":"DrawContext.text tessellates glyph outlines every frame, so a canvas of text is slow","message":"Reported by the RayDE session building a terminal view, and seen again in the banner's gauges.\n\n**What was measured**\n\nA 117 by 23 screen drawn as one `ctx.text` per styled run, 473 runs, took 44 ms to encode a frame even when the same lines repeat. The profile is `CanvasPainter.tessellate` and `build` on the CPU (`ArrayBytes_Float.push`, `Bytes.setFloat`): the glyph paths are tessellated again each frame. RayDE moved the text to retained `\u003ctext\u003e` elements placed by column, rebuilding only the changed rows; the same screen costs 2.6 ms a frame, 1.2 ms of it the flush. In a page the banner's canvases, almost all text and paths, make a frame 90 to 160 ms when they redraw every frame.\n\n**What is wanted**\n\n`ctx.text` drawing from the glyph atlas, or caching a glyph's tessellation across frames, so a canvas can host text cheaply.","files":null}]}